          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">August 31, 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="13.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#by-system" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Lifespan by System</a>
          <a href="#what-kills-it" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. What Actually Kills Epoxy Floors</a>
          <a href="#extend" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Making It Last Longer</a>
          <a href="#signs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Signs It Is Failing</a>
          <a href="#vs-alternatives" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Against the Alternatives</a>
          <a href="#when-to-recoat" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Recoating vs Replacing</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Epoxy floors do not usually wear out. They come off — and they come off because the concrete underneath was never opened up properly to bond to. Which is why the honest answer to how long a floor lasts starts with a question about the grinder, not the product.
        </p>

        <div id="by-system" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Long Each Type of Epoxy Floor Lasts
          </h2>

          <p>
            The spread between the cheapest and the best garage floor coating is enormous, and most of it is explained by film thickness and surface preparation rather than by brand.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">System</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Lifespan</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Is</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Big-box water-based kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-5 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thin film, acid etch prep, single coat</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">High-solids DIY kit with topcoat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8-12 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thicker build, separate clear topcoat</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Professional epoxy, roller applied</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-15 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ground slab, less uniform film than spray</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Professional epoxy with polyaspartic topcoat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15-25 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ground slab, flake broadcast, UV-stable wear layer</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Garage floor paint (for comparison)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not a coating system; wears rather than bonds</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The gap between the first and last rows is roughly a factor of five, and the products in those rows are not five times different in chemistry. What differs is how thick the finished film is and whether the concrete was mechanically profiled before coating. Both of those are decisions, not properties of the product in the tin.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Number That Predicts Everything</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Preparation, not product, decides the outcome.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A mid-range epoxy over a properly diamond-ground slab will comfortably outlast a premium system rolled onto concrete that was merely swept and acid-etched. If you are weighing spending more on the kit against spending more on preparation, preparation wins every time.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="what-kills-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Actually Ends an Epoxy Floor’s Life
          </h2>

          <p>
            Epoxy floors rarely fail by gradually wearing thin the way a driveway sealer does. They fail through specific mechanisms, and knowing them tells you what to guard against.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Delamination From Poor Preparation</h3>

          <p>
            By far the most common cause of premature failure. Concrete straight from the trowel is comparatively closed and often carries laitance, curing compounds or old sealer on the surface. Epoxy needs an open, profiled surface to key into mechanically. Applied over a closed slab, it forms a film that is sitting on the concrete rather than bonded into it, and it lifts in sheets — often starting where a car tyre sits.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hot Tire Pickup</h3>

          <p>
            A tyre that has been driven on a hot day retains a great deal of heat and puts it into a small contact patch. That heat softens an inadequately cured or thin epoxy, and the tyre bonds to it. When the car pulls away the coating lifts with the tread. This is the classic failure of thin water-based kits and of floors that were driven on before full chemical cure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Moisture Vapour From Below</h3>

          <p>
            A slab without an effective vapour barrier beneath it continually transmits moisture upward. That vapour cannot pass through a film coating, so pressure builds at the bond line and pushes the coating off, usually as bubbles or blisters that later break open. No coating quality overcomes this — it has to be tested for and addressed before coating.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">UV Ambering</h3>

          <p>
            Standard epoxy is not UV stable and yellows where direct sunlight reaches it, often within two to four years. It is a cosmetic rather than structural failure, but it is permanent, and on a floor that otherwise had a decade left it is frequently what prompts a recoat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Slab Movement</h3>

          <p>
            Cured epoxy is rigid. If a hairline crack in the slab opens, or the slab settles slightly, the coating has little capacity to accommodate that and telegraphs or splits along the line. A flexible topcoat mitigates this; nothing eliminates it on a slab that is genuinely moving.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="extend" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How to Get the Long End of the Range
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every lever is pulled before or during installation. Once the floor is down, maintenance protects it but cannot add years that the preparation did not build in.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">1</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Test for moisture before anything else</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Tape a plastic sheet down overnight and check for condensation underneath, or use a calcium chloride test. A slab with active vapour transmission needs addressing first, not coating.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">2</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Mechanically profile the slab</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Diamond grinding or shot blasting, not acid etching. This is the highest-leverage decision in the entire project and the one most DIY kits gloss over.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">3</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Repair cracks and honour control joints</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Route and fill cracks properly. Coating straight over a control joint invites the coating to split exactly where the slab is designed to move.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">4</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Remove every trace of oil contamination</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Degrease and, for soaked-in stains, use a dedicated treatment. Epoxy will not bond over petroleum residue and will lift there first.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">5</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Choose a high-solids product over a water-based kit</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Solids content determines film thickness, and film thickness determines resistance to hot tyres and abrasion.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">6</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Apply a UV-stable topcoat</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A polyaspartic or polyurea clear coat prevents ambering and adds the abrasion resistance that keeps the floor looking new.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">7</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Respect the full cure before parking on it</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Walk-on at 18 to 24 hours does not mean drive-on. Give it the full label cure, commonly a week, before a vehicle returns.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Maintenance That Actually Matters</h3>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Sweep or dust-mop regularly; grit underfoot is what abrades the finish.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Wipe up oil, brake fluid and battery acid promptly rather than letting them sit.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Use furniture pads under jack stands, tool chests and anything with a hard point load.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Avoid dragging metal shelving and equipment across the surface.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Rinse off road salt in winter, which is corrosive and works into any breach in the film.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Tell Your Epoxy Floor Is Coming to the End
          </h2>

          <p>
            Epoxy tends to give warning before it fails comprehensively. These are the signals, roughly in order of seriousness.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What You See</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Means</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">How Urgent</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dulling and loss of gloss</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Normal wear of the top surface</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low; a recoat restores it</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Yellow cast in sunlit areas</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">UV ambering of a non-stable epoxy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cosmetic; recoat with a UV-stable topcoat</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fine scratches in traffic paths</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Abrasion from grit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low; maintenance issue</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bubbles or blisters</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moisture vapour pushing from below</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High; investigate before recoating</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Peeling at edges or tyre positions</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bond failure or hot tire pickup</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High; the film is not anchored</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Coating lifting in sheets</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Delamination from poor preparation</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Terminal for that area</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Dulling and light scratching are maintenance-stage problems and a scuff-and-recoat with a fresh topcoat can add years cheaply. Peeling and blistering are different in kind — they indicate the bond or the slab itself is the problem, and recoating over them simply adds a new layer to something that is already lifting.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Recoat Early, Not Late</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A worn topcoat is cheap to renew; a delaminated base coat is not.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If the wear is confined to the clear topcoat, scuffing and applying a fresh coat is a modest job that resets the clock. Once wear has gone through to the base coat or the concrete, you are into removal and full reinstallation. Inspect annually and recoat while the damage is still in the top layer.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Epoxy Lifespan Compares to the Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            Lifespan is only meaningful against what else you could have put down, and the cost-per-year comparison is often more revealing than the headline price.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Option</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Lifespan</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Garage floor paint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cheapest; wears rather than bonds, needs frequent redoing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">DIY water-based epoxy kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-5 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thin film; vulnerable to hot tire pickup</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">High-solids epoxy with topcoat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8-12 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The DIY sweet spot for cost against life</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Professional epoxy plus polyaspartic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15-25 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Highest upfront cost, lowest cost per year</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Interlocking tiles</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-20 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No cure time, hides slab defects, seams collect debris</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Spread over their service lives, the expensive professional system frequently works out cheaper per year than repeatedly redoing a budget kit, and considerably cheaper than paint. That calculation only holds if you intend to stay in the house long enough to realise it, which is the genuine argument for a mid-range DIY approach in a home you may sell within a few years.
          </p>

          <p>
            One factor worth weighing beyond arithmetic: redoing a garage floor is disruptive. The garage has to be emptied, ground, coated and left to cure, which is a week of lost use each time. A floor that lasts twenty years costs you that disruption once rather than four times.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-recoat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. When to Recoat and When to Start Over
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most epoxy floors reach a point where something needs doing, and the choice between a modest topcoat renewal and a full removal is worth getting right, because the cost difference is large.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Recoat If</h3>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>The gloss has dulled and there are fine traffic scratches, but the film is intact everywhere.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>The colour has ambered in sunlit areas but nothing is lifting.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Wear is confined to the clear topcoat and has not reached the base coat or flake layer.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>There are no blisters, and no peeling at tyre positions or edges.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            A recoat means scuff-sanding the surface to give the new material tooth, cleaning thoroughly, and applying a fresh clear topcoat. It is a weekend job, it restores appearance and abrasion resistance, and it effectively resets the wear clock on the floor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Start Over If</h3>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>The coating is lifting in sheets or peeling back from edges and tyre positions.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>There are blisters or bubbles, which indicate moisture vapour rather than surface wear.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Traffic has worn through to bare concrete in places, leaving two substrates.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>The coating has cracked along slab cracks or control joints that were coated over.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            These are bond or substrate failures, and a new coat over them inherits the problem. The floor needs grinding back and reinstalling on a properly prepared and, where moisture is involved, properly addressed slab.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical advice is to inspect annually and to act at the first sign of dulling rather than waiting for damage. Recoating early is cheap and easy; recoating late is not an option at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            7. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Acid Etching Instead of Grinding</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Acid etching is what most consumer kits recommend because it requires no equipment, and it is the leading reason those floors last three to five years rather than ten. It does not produce a consistent mechanical profile, particularly on dense or power-trowelled concrete. Diamond grinding or shot blasting is what a long-lived floor is built on.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Parking on It Before Full Cure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Walk-on at 18 to 24 hours is not drive-on. A tyre carrying road heat onto an incompletely cured film softens it and bonds to it, lifting the coating away when the car moves. Give the floor its full chemical cure, commonly seven days, before a vehicle returns.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating a Slab With Active Moisture</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Vapour rising through a slab has nowhere to go once a film coating is over it, so it collects at the bond line and pushes the coating off as blisters. No product quality overcomes this. Test with a taped plastic sheet or a calcium chloride kit before you buy anything.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the UV-Stable Topcoat in a Sunlit Garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Standard epoxy ambers where direct sun reaches it, typically within two to four years, and the change is permanent. On a floor that structurally had a decade left, yellowing is often what triggers an early redo. A polyaspartic or polyurea clear coat prevents it and adds abrasion resistance.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Waiting Until the Base Coat Is Exposed to Recoat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Wear confined to the clear topcoat can be scuffed and recoated cheaply, resetting the floor’s clock. Once traffic has gone through to the base coat or bare concrete, the job becomes removal and full reinstallation. Inspect annually and act while the damage is still in the top layer.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a garage epoxy floor last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends heavily on the system. A big-box water-based kit typically lasts three to five years, a high-solids DIY kit with a separate topcoat eight to twelve, and a professionally installed epoxy floor with a polyaspartic topcoat fifteen to twenty-five. Surface preparation explains more of that spread than the product does.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why did my epoxy garage floor peel after a year?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always inadequate surface preparation. Epoxy needs an open, mechanically profiled slab to key into, and applied over closed, sealed or contaminated concrete it forms a film sitting on top rather than bonded in. Hot tire pickup and moisture vapour from below are the other two common early failures.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does epoxy flooring wear out or come off?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It usually comes off rather than wearing through. The common end-of-life mechanisms are delamination from poor preparation, hot tire pickup, blistering from moisture vapour, and cracking where the slab moves. Genuine abrasive wear-through happens mainly on thin coatings in heavy traffic.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How can I make my epoxy garage floor last longer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most of it is decided at installation: mechanically grind rather than acid etch, test for moisture first, repair cracks, use a high-solids product and add a UV-stable topcoat. Afterwards, sweep grit regularly, wipe up oil promptly, use pads under point loads, and rinse road salt off in winter.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you recoat an epoxy garage floor instead of replacing it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, provided the failure is confined to the clear topcoat. Scuff-sand the surface and apply a fresh topcoat and the floor is effectively reset, which is a modest job. If the coating is peeling, blistering or lifting in sheets, the bond has failed and recoating over it just adds a layer to something already coming off.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a professional epoxy floor worth the extra cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a cost-per-year basis usually yes — fifteen to twenty-five years against three to five for a budget kit often makes the expensive system cheaper annually, and it saves repeating a disruptive week-long job. The argument weakens if you may sell within a few years, where a high-solids DIY kit with a topcoat is the sensible middle ground.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

