        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Three to five years for a big-box water-based kit, eight to twelve for a high-solids kit with a proper topcoat, and fifteen to twenty-five for a professionally installed system with a polyaspartic wear layer. That is a fivefold spread, and the chemistry in the tin explains surprisingly little of it.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            What explains it is preparation and film thickness. Epoxy floors mostly do not wear out — they delaminate, because the concrete was acid-etched rather than ground and the coating never keyed into the slab. If your budget forces a choice between a better product and better preparation, choose preparation without hesitating.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then protect what you built. Test for moisture before you start, respect the full cure before parking on it, add a UV-stable topcoat if any sunlight reaches the floor, and recoat while the wear is still confined to that top layer. A topcoat renewal is a weekend; a delaminated floor is a removal job and a fresh start.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
